= August 2027 — The laws survive; the label lands

President **[+ president_name +]** has vetoed **[+
sikorski_left_veto_count +] repeal bills**. None of those vetoes writes a new
law or restores a statute already lost: each simply prevents Sikorski's Sejm
majority from erasing a reform that had reached the statute book.

The cabinet answers with a disciplined campaign. Every stalled appointment,
delayed permit and confrontation outside Parliament is attributed to *the veto
President*. KO and Konfederacja disagree about almost everything except the
advertisement. The phrase hurts **[+ left_party_name +]** in the polls even as
the abortion, marriage and labour settlements protected by the Palace remain
law. Ministries can still obstruct their implementation; they cannot pretend
the President signed their repeal.

- @sikorski_veto_own_2027
- @sikorski_veto_constitution_2027
- @sikorski_veto_distance_2027

@sikorski_veto_own_2027
title: Own the veto wall
subtitle: Accept the polling cost and make preservation of the reforms the Left's closing identity.
on-arrival: party_unity += 4; progressive_credibility += 5; feminist_trust += 4; labor_credibility += 3; left_poll_momentum -= 0.10; public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_salience += 10; public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_support += -5; news_headline = "The Left embraces the veto wall and the electoral cost attached to it"

We stop apologising for the veto wall and start campaigning on it. Every
preserved statute is named: the abortion law that still stands, the marriages
still lawful, the labour code Sikorski could not repeal. If the accusation is
that a Left President stopped this government, the answer is yes.

The polling cost is real and arrives immediately. So does something the party
has not had for years — a closing argument that its own organisers believe
without qualification.

- @poland_event_queue: Name every preserved statute

@sikorski_veto_constitution_2027
title: Publish the constitutional and implementation ledger
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Separate lawful vetoes from ministerial sabotage line by line.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The legal and administrative audit cannot be financed.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; constitutional_restraint += 5; judicial_legitimacy += 4; media_capacity += 3; public_trust += 2; news_headline = "The Palace vetoes and the cabinet's implementation sabotage enter one public ledger"

The ledger goes out line by line: on one side every veto, with its
constitutional basis and the article it rests on; on the other every
appointment frozen, every guidance withdrawn and every budget line quietly
unspent by a ministry that lost the vote and governed anyway.

It is a document for people who read documents. Judges' associations,
the Commission and three serious newspapers treat it as definitive; the
evening broadcasts continue to say *veto President*, because that is two words
and this is forty pages.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the ledger

@sikorski_veto_distance_2027
title: Put distance between the party and the Palace
subtitle: Recover some campaign room by insisting that the President—not the party—owns each veto.
on-arrival: left_poll_momentum += 0.15; president_relation -= 8; party_unity -= 3; progressive_credibility -= 3; coalition_blur += 2; news_headline = "The Left separates its campaign from the veto President"

We say, accurately, that the President exercises his own constitutional
judgment and that a party does not hold the pen. It buys back some room in the
places where the obstruction charge has landed hardest.

The Palace hears it as abandonment, which it partly is. Our own members hear a
leadership putting distance between itself and the only institution that
preserved the reforms they spent eight years winning, and several of them say
so publicly before the week is out.

- @poland_event_queue: Put the distance on the record

